s="\"Support Equalities Among Ribbon Schur Functions (https://wjhardt.github.io/support_equalities.pdf)\\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; implies that two ribbons can have the same Schur support only if one is obtained ... for a ribbon α to have full equivalence class. ... necessary condition, which we conjecture to be suﬃcient.\\nSupport Equalities Among Ribbon Schur Functions\\nMarisa Gaetz\\nWill Hardt\\nMassachusetts Institute of Technology\\nCambridge, MA, U.S.A.\\nUniversity of Wisconsin – Madison\\nMadison, WI, U.S.A.\\nmgaetz@mit.edu\\nwhardt@wisc.edu\\nShruthi Sridhar\\nPrinceton University\\nPrinceton, NJ, U.S.A.\\nssridhar@math.princeton.edu\\nSubmitted: Oct 8, 2018; Accepted: Aug 18, 2019; Published: Sep 13, 2019\\n© The authors. Released under the CC BY license (International 4.0).\\nAbstract\\nIn 2007, McNamara proved that two skew shapes can have the same Schur\\nsupport only if they have the same number of k ×ℓrectangles as subdiagrams. This\\nimplies that two ribbons can have the same Schur support only if one is obtained\\nby permuting row lengths of the other. We present substantial progress towards\\nclassifying when a permutation π ∈Sm of row lengths of a ribbon α produces a\\nribbon απ with the same Schur support as α; when this occurs for all π ∈Sm, we\\nsay that α has full equivalence class. Our main results include a suﬃcient condition\\nfor a ribbon α to have full equivalence class. Additionally, we prove a separate\\nnecessary condition, which we conjecture to be suﬃcient.\\nMathematics Subject Classiﬁcations: 05E05\\n1\\nIntroduction\\nThe question of when two skew diagrams yield equal skew Schur functions has been\\nstudied in detail. For instance, this question is addressed by Billera, Thomas, and van\\nWilligenburg in [2], by McNamara and van Willigenburg in [8], and by Reiner, Shaw,\\nand van Willigenburg in [10]. In this paper, we consider the related question of when\\ntwo skew diagrams yield skew Schur functions such that when expanded, the same set of\\nSchur functions appear with nonzero coeﬃcient. In other words, we consider the question\\nof when two skew diagrams have equal Schur support (Deﬁnition 1). This question has\\nreceived less attention, with the most substantial progress coming from McNamara in\\n2007 [6] and from McNamara and van Willigenburg in 2011 [7].\\nthe electronic journal of combinatorics 26(3) (2019), #P3.52\\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nEquality of Schur (https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~reiner/REU/REU2017notes/problem-1-presentation.pdf)\\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Ribbons ... α has full equivalence class, then N < Pm α −(m −j −2) for ... Conjecture\\nThe above necessary condition is sufficient for a ribbon to have full\\n| Necessary Condition for Full E.C\\n--- | ---\\nEquality of Schur\\nSupports of\\nRibbons\\nGaetz, Hardt,\\nSridhar, Tran\\nPreliminaries\\nMain Results\\nOther Results\\nAcknowledgements\\nReferences  | Theorem (S, G, H, Tran, ’17)\\nLet α = (α 1, α 2, . . . , α m) be a ribbon, where α ≥α ≥· · · ≥α m. If\\n1 2\\nα has full equivalence class, then N < Pm α −(m −j −2) for\\nj i=j+1 i\\nall j ≤m −2, where\\nN = max{k| X (k −α i) ≤m −j −2}.\\nj\\ni≤j: αi<k\\nWe prove the contrapositive by assuming\\nm\\nN ≥ X α −(m −j −2)\\nj i\\ni=j+1\\nand showing that there exists a content for an LR-filling of α\\n(j j+1)\\nthat is not the content of any LR-filling of α.\\nConjecture\\nThe above necessary condition is sufficient for a ribbon to have full\\nequivalence class.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nSupportEqualitiesAmongRibbonSchurFunctions (https://web.math.princeton.edu/~ssridhar/research/posters/SchurPoster.pdf)\\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; We conjecture that our necessary condition is also sufﬁcient. ... A ribbon α = (α1, α2, . . . , αm) is said to have full equivalence class if ... Question: Which connected ribbons have full equivalence class?\\nSupportEqualitiesAmongRibbonSchurFunctions\\nMarisa Gaetz\\nMassachusetts Institute of Technology\\nWill Hardt\\nCarleton College\\nShruthi Sridhar\\nCornell University\\nINTRODUCTION\\nQUESTION\\nNECESSARY CONDITION\\nLet α = (α1, α2, . . . , αm) be a ribbon. Let απ denote a ribbon formed\\nby applying the permutation π ∈Sm to the row lengths of α.\\nLet α = (α1, α2, . . . , αm) be a ribbon, where α1 ≥α2 ≥· · · ≥αm. If\\nα has full equivalence class, then Nj < Pm\\ni=j+1 αi −(m −j −2) for\\nall j ≤m −2, where\\nα =\\nα(2 3) =\\nα(1 2) =\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nNj = max\\ni≤j,αi<k\\n(k −αi) < m −j −2\\nk\\n\\f\\f\\f\\nX\\n\\nα(1 3 2) =\\nα(1 2 3) =\\nα(1 3) =\\nIn [1], McNamara proved that two skew diagrams can have the same\\nSchur support only if they have the same number of k×ℓrectangles\\nas subdiagrams. It follows that two connected ribbons α and β can\\nhave the same Schur support only if one is obtained by permuting\\nrow lengths of the other (i.e. β = απ for some permutation π). We\\ngive a necessary and a sufﬁcient condition for an m-rowed ribbon α\\nto have the same Schur support as every permutation απ, for π ∈\\nSm. We conjecture that our necessary condition is also sufﬁcient.\\nConjecture: This necessary condition is sufﬁcient as well.\\nA ribbon α = (α1, α2, . . . , αm) is said to have full equivalence class if\\nfor all permutations π ∈Sm, we have [α] = [απ].\\nPRELIMINARIES\\nQuestion: Which connected ribbons have full equivalence class?\\nNote: A weaker but simpler version of our necessary condition is:\\nαi < Pm\\nk=i+1 αk for all 1 ≤i ≤m −2.\\nA Young diagram of a partition λ = (λ1, λ2, . . . , λm) is a collection\\nof rows of left aligned boxes, where row i has length λi.\\nSUFFICIENT CONDITION\\nPROOF SKETCH\\n• If the jth necessary inequality is not satisﬁed for a ribbon α,\\nwe can use the LR-Rule to show that [α(j j+1)] ̸= [α].\\nLet α = (α1, α2, . .